The bug is fixed. Build passes (`BUILD SUCCESSFUL`).

## Diagnosis

`ManualEntryDialog.java` parsed raw user input with `Integer.parseInt` / `Long.parseLong` and no `NumberFormatException` guard, in two places:

- **TOTP crash** — the `TextWatcher.afterTextChanged` validator (lines 282/293) parsed Digits and Period on *every keystroke*. Typing `3000000000` overflowed `Integer.MAX_VALUE` and threw before the Save button could ever be disabled.
- **HOTP crash** — the Save button's `onClick` (line 245) parsed Counter with `Long.parseLong`. `1000000000000000000000` overflowed `Long.MAX_VALUE`.

Note the `TextUtils.isEmpty(...)` pre-checks only covered the *empty* case, never the *out-of-range* case — that gap is the actual defect.

## Patch

**1. Added two null-returning parse helpers** (`parseIntegerOrNull`, `parseLongOrNull`) that convert an unparseable field into a `null` sentinel instead of a thrown exception.

**2. Rewrote the validator** to treat `null` as invalid, which implements the behavior the report asks for — an out-of-range number now *disables* Save rather than crashing:
```java
Integer digits = parseIntegerOrNull(digitsInput);
if (... || digits == null || digits == 0) {
    positiveButton.setEnabled(false);
} else if (type == Entry.OTPType.HOTP) {
    positiveButton.setEnabled(parseLongOrNull(counterInput) != null);
} else if (type == TOTP || type == STEAM) {
    Integer period = parseIntegerOrNull(periodInput);
    positiveButton.setEnabled(period != null && period != 0);
}
```
The helpers subsume the old `TextUtils.isEmpty` checks (empty input parses to `null`), so empty-field behavior is unchanged.

**3. Hardened the save handler** with defense-in-depth — each of digits/period/counter bails out with an inline `setError` instead of propagating the exception. This matters because the watcher only fires on text edits; the Save path shouldn't rely on it having run.

**4. Added string** `error_invalid_number` ("Invalid number") to `values/strings_main.xml`, alongside the existing `error_invalid_secret`. Only the default locale is added — translations fall back to it automatically.

One incidental improvement: a lone `-` (permitted by `android:inputType="number"`) also used to crash the watcher and is now handled by the same path.

